// =================================================================================================
// SITE-67 ART KIT — Ozea. **This is the shipped kit** (`site::kit::SITE_KIT_PATH`).
//
// `kit_greybox.ron` remains beside it as the swap fixture, and the pair is the proof the seam works:
// `site::kit::tests::the_site_kit_is_swappable_by_authoring_one_file` loads both and asserts the
// Site's look — and its SCALES — differ with zero code edits. Before 2026-08-01 the kit was a `match`
// in Rust and re-skinning the Site meant recompiling it.
//
// HEIGHT is the mesh's **authored** height in metres; the scale applied is `target / authored`
// (`pieces::target_height` is the game's policy, this file is the art's fact). Every number below was
// MEASURED by `scripts/fbx_to_glb.py`, which writes an `INVENTORY.md` beside the staged GLBs with each
// mesh's W/H/D — never hand-guessed, and never re-derived from a mesh AABB at load, which would read
// 100× large because the library is authored in centimetres.
//
// ── Every mesh here is base-at-0, and the wall family is authored to 2.40 ──
//
// Both were fixed on 2026-08-01 after the player reported *"the north and south wall doesn't square
// into the east-and-west walls… looks like the floors point of origin may be off too."*
//
//   * **Origin.** 11 of the 16 meshes used to carry whatever pivot their source pack happened to have
//     — walls and floors centre-origined, props base-origined. `y_scale` is applied about the entity
//     origin, so a centre-origined 2.00 m wall scaled by 1.2 became Y[-1.2,+1.2]: half of it
//     underground and 1.17 m standing against 2.40 m intended. `assets/ozea/README.md` has the full
//     account; `tests/ozea_asset.rs` now pins the convention against the bytes.
//   * **Height.** `wall`, `wall_corner`, `wall_window` and `column` are re-authored to a true 2.40 m
//     by `scripts/ozea_wall_heights.py` — cut at a plain band and lengthened, so trim and skirting
//     keep their authored proportions. Their `y_scale` is now a no-op **1.0**. This replaces a 1.2×
//     stretch on the walls and a **2.18×** stretch on the column, the very distortion
//     `assets/config/config.ron` cites as the reason the Ozea walls were never promoted into the
//     dungeon furniture kit.
//
// `wall_low` is the one piece still scaled — 2.00 authored against a 0.9 target, a 0.45× squash. It is
// knowingly left, because shortening a panel properly is an authoring decision, not a conversion flag.
//
// ── Why these meshes and not the obvious ones ──
//
// The Site grid is 1 m and `site::visuals::place` scales **only Y** — X and Z are hard 1.0, so a
// mesh's footprint is used as authored. That single fact chose most of this file:
//
//   * `SM_Wall_Straight`, the natural pick, is **2.00 m long** and would span two cells. `SM_Wall_1x2`
//     is the same kit's 1.00 m run and is the one that lands on the grid.
//   * `SM_Wall_CornerCap` (0.22 m footprint) is the corner POST, not a 2 m corner panel — modular
//     corners here are two runs meeting behind a post.
//   * The floors are exactly 1.00 × 1.00, which is why they were the only pieces the earlier partial
//     kit dared adopt.
//
// The two doorway pieces are deliberately NOT on the 1 m grid, and that is correct rather than a
// compromise: `pieces::SitePiece::WallDoorway` is documented as a **2 m opening**, so a 2.06 m frame
// is the intended size.
//
// ⚠️ Do NOT map containment cells from `Cellule`. `docs/2026-07-26-site-hub-and-operative-knowledge.md`
// §7 measured it at 0.30 × 0.50 × 0.30 m — French *cellule* is a **battery** cell, not a room. The
// real article is `Cryogenic_Stasis_Chamber`, promoted below as `cryo_pod.glb`, and its 1.00 × 1.02
// footprint drops onto a single grid cell.
// =================================================================================================
(
    // ── Structure ──
    // 1.00 × 1.00 floor plate, so one mesh is one cell.
    //
    // `y_offset: -0.06` sinks the plate so its TOP face lands exactly on y = 0. That plane is not
    // arbitrary: it is where avatar feet stand, where `command_avatar` resolves a click order, and
    // where `nav` puts every cell centre. Without this the plate's top would sit at +0.06 and
    // everything that walks on it would stand 6 cm inside it. The greybox kit gets this for free —
    // its floor is a zero-thickness plane already at y = 0.
    floor:             (glb: "ozea/floor.glb",                height: 0.06, y_offset: -0.06),
    // 1.00 m run (`SM_Wall_1x2`), authored to a true 2.40 m — see the header note on heights.
    wall:              (glb: "ozea/wall.glb",                 height: 2.40),
    // The corner POST — see the header note.
    wall_corner:       (glb: "ozea/wall_corner.glb",          height: 2.40),
    // ── The two doorways, and the OPENING each actually leaves ──
    //
    // `opening` is the clear hole: jamb inner face to jamb inner face, floor to lintel underside. It
    // is NOT derivable from `height`, and it is what the ASYNC aperture quad is built from. Measured
    // from each mesh's POSITION accessors — for `doorframe_double` the jambs face each other at
    // |z| = 0.800 and the lintel underside sits at y = 1.626, with small plinths at the jamb feet
    // (|z| 0.646–0.715, up to y 0.203) that crop the quad's bottom corners, which is wanted.
    //
    // Until 2026-08-01 the quad was sized from `door.trigger_half_extents` instead — a GAMEPLAY
    // volume, generous on purpose because it must catch a walking avatar — giving a 3.2 m quad
    // against this 1.6 m hole. The aperture material is opaque by design, so that overhang punched
    // through the wall either side of the door rather than fading out.
    wall_doorway:      (mesh: (glb: "ozea/doorframe_single.glb", height: 2.01), opening: (0.867, 1.705)),
    // The ASYNC aperture's frame. The one mesh promoted before this pass.
    wall_doorway_wide: (mesh: (glb: "ozea/doorframe_double.glb", height: 1.98), opening: (1.600, 1.626)),
    // The header course over a doorway: `WALL_HEIGHT` 2.40 − `DOORWAY_HEIGHT` 2.00 = a 0.40 m band.
    // Without it the perimeter had a 0.40 × 2.00 m slot straight through it above the ASYNC door.
    //
    // Built by CROPPING the top 0.40 m off `SM_Wall_1x2` (`scripts/ozea_wall_heights.py`'s `HEADERS`),
    // not by squashing a wall. A header course IS the top of the wall, so the crop keeps the wall's
    // own top trim at its authored size and the piece lines up with the wall tops either side of the
    // door by construction. `y_scale` is a no-op 1.0.
    wall_header:       (glb: "ozea/wall_header.glb",          height: 0.40),
    // Containment-cell glazing — the player must be able to SEE what is held.
    wall_window:       (glb: "ozea/wall_window.glb",          height: 2.40),
    // Waist-high run: the Records desk and the Requisition counter. Authored 2.00 m and squashed to
    // `target_height` 0.9 — the one piece in this kit whose scale is a real compromise rather than a
    // fit, kept because a counter is the cheapest way to furnish a room and this is the only panel
    // in the promoted set with a 1 m footprint.
    wall_low:          (glb: "ozea/wall_low.glb",             height: 2.00),
    // `SM_Pylonne`, authored 1.10 m in the pack and re-authored to 2.40 here. It was the worst
    // offender of the lot: at its native height `y_scale` stretched it **2.18×**.
    column:            (glb: "ozea/column.glb",               height: 2.40),

    // ── Dressing ──
    crate_:            (glb: "ozea/crate.glb",                height: 1.00),
    pipe:              (glb: "ozea/pipe.glb",                 height: 0.30),
    pipe_corner:       (glb: "ozea/pipe_corner.glb",          height: 0.30),
    // ── Floor inlays ──
    // `y_offset` on all three, and it is not optional: these are the same 0.06/0.05 m thickness as the
    // floor plate they lie on, so with both seated at y = 0 their top faces would be coplanar and the
    // depth winner undefined — it renders as white squares flickering against grey ones, which is
    // exactly what the player captured on 2026-08-01. See `KitPiece::y_offset`.
    //
    // **0.002, not 0.062.** The old value cleared a plate whose top sat at +0.03 because the mesh was
    // centre-origined; now every mesh is base-at-0 and `floor` is sunk to put its top on y = 0, so an
    // inlay only has to clear the *plane*. 2 mm, the same margin as before.
    //
    // The lit threshold pad at the aperture's mouth.
    floor_button:      (glb: "ozea/floor_light.glb",          height: 0.06, y_offset: 0.002),
    // Wing colour-coding: learnability without signage (design doc §2.4).
    area_decal:        (glb: "ozea/floor_line_cross.glb",     height: 0.05, y_offset: 0.002),
    arrow_decal:       (glb: "ozea/floor_line_straight.glb",  height: 0.05, y_offset: 0.002),

    // ── The specimen ──
    // No longer a stand-in. `fill_containment_cells` puts one of these in a cell per held specimen,
    // in capture order — so what the player walks past is a rack of cryo pods holding the things they
    // brought home, which is the entire argument for the hub being a place rather than a menu.
    specimen_standin:  (glb: "ozea/cryo_pod.glb",             height: 2.41),
)
